 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "in-plain-english",
   "heading": "In plain English",
   "role": "intuition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "in-plain-english-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Any symmetric matrix can be understood as a pure stretch along a set of perpendicular directions. The spectral decomposition writes it that way: rotate into those directions, stretch each by its eigenvalue, rotate back. In that form, raising the matrix to a power, inverting it or taking its square root is easy — you just do it to each stretch factor."
      },
      {
       "t": "callout",
       "kind": "why",
       "title": "Why it matters",
       "md": "It is how generalised least squares transforms data to fix unequal or correlated errors, and the basis of principal components.",
       "folded": false
      }
     ],
     "words": 83,
     "symbols": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "definition",
   "heading": "Definition",
   "role": "definition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "definition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For any **symmetric** $A$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "A = Q\\Lambda Q', \\qquad Q'Q = QQ' = I"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Columns of $Q$ are orthonormal eigenvectors; $\\Lambda$ is the diagonal matrix of (real) eigenvalues."
      }
     ],
     "words": 15,
     "symbols": [
      "$A$",
      "$Q$",
      "$\\Lambda$",
      "$I$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "intuition",
   "heading": "Intuition",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "intuition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "In the eigenvector coordinate system, a symmetric matrix just stretches each axis by its eigenvalue. $Q'$ rotates into those coordinates, $\\Lambda$ stretches, $Q$ rotates back."
      }
     ],
     "words": 22,
     "symbols": [
      "$Q$",
      "$\\Lambda$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "what-it-buys-you",
   "heading": "What it buys you",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "what-it-buys-you-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Functions of $A$ act on the eigenvalues only:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "A^k = Q\\Lambda^kQ', \\qquad A^{-1} = Q\\Lambda^{-1}Q', \\qquad A^{1/2} = Q\\Lambda^{1/2}Q'"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "**Worked example**: for $A = \\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$ (eigenvalues 3 and 1),"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "A^{-1/2} = Q\\,\\mathrm{diag}(3^{-1/2}, 1)\\,Q' = \\begin{pmatrix}0.789&-0.211\\\\-0.211&0.789\\end{pmatrix}"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "and $A^{-1/2}AA^{-1/2} = I$."
      }
     ],
     "words": 15,
     "symbols": [
      "$A$",
      "$Q$",
      "$\\Lambda$",
      "$I$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "where-it-is-used",
   "heading": "Where it is used",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "where-it-is-used-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Econometrics**: GLS transforms the model by $\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}^{-1/2}$ so that errors become homoskedastic and uncorrelated — this is how [Homoskedasticity](note:homoskedasticity) failures are fixed efficiently; showing $\\mathbf{M}$ is a projection with $n-K$ unit eigenvalues ([Projection Matrix and Residual Maker](note:projection-matrix-and-residual-maker)).\n- **Statistics**: principal components are the eigenvectors of a covariance matrix."
      }
     ],
     "words": 45,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\boldsymbol{\\Omega}$",
      "$\\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{P}$",
      "$n$, $K$"
     ]
    }
   ]
